ue] [17592186283090 1113 #uuid "5f7d83bc-e215-56af-9039-1143690e2108" 13194139534268 true] [17592186283090 1145 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-6x){e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-6x){e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ 3x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=0$ and $x=2.$" 13194139534268 true] [17592186283091 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283092 13194139534268 true] [17592186283091 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283093 13194139534268 true] [17592186283091 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283094 13194139534268 true] [17592186283091 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283095 13194139534268 true] [17592186283091 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283096 13194139534268 true] [17592186283091 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534268 true] [17592186283091 1041 "selection" 13194139534268 true] [17592186283091 1104 #edb/ref 17592186283092 13194139534268 true] [17592186283091 1110 #uuid "7b1e0662-f7c3-5743-b6d9-4a287a964e1f" 13194139534268 true] [17592186283092 1034 "$x=0$ and $x=2$" 13194139534268 true] [17592186283092 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534268 